        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Why does Wasserstein GAN deserve its own place in the history of artificial intelligence?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial-hook"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Wasserstein GAN replaced the original GAN divergence with a distance inspired by optimal transport. The critic objective made training behavior easier to interpret and helped diagnose mode collapse and convergence problems.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-wasserstein-generative-adversarial-networks"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.historical-background"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "A critic estimates how far the generated distribution is from the real distribution, giving smoother feedback to the generator.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-wasserstein-generative-adversarial-networks"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.core-idea"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Experts generally treat Wasserstein GAN as a conceptual advance in understanding why GAN training can fail. Its long-term legacy is the use of transport-inspired distances and critic constraints in generative modeling.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-wasserstein-generative-adversarial-networks"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.long-term-legacy"
        },
